  },
  {
    "episode_id": 45973,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45973.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it could be, it could be both. You know, we have certain people that are uncomfortable with digital assets that may be trying to gain control by the federal government over digital assets. And we may have people at Treasury who are just trying to bulk up what we call the pay fors within the bill, trying to make sure that they can say that this bill is paid for. They were coming up with numbers that they thought they could obtain in tax revenue from cryptocurrency transactions in excess of $30 billion and were using that as a score or a pay for in the bill. And by our understanding of the bill, that's dramatically overstating. the amount of money that could be obtained through this bill from transactions and digital assets. So we're trying to weigh in on this bill in a bipartisan way. We're working with Senator Wyden from Oregon. We're working with Senator Toomey from Pennsylvania. Senator Cruz from Texas has taken an interest in this subject. Certainly, Marcia Blackburn is interested in this subject, Kirsten Cinema, who's one of the leaders of the effort to shape this bill, is also my co-chair on the financial innovation caucus. So we have some people who are friendly to a regulatory environment that allows digital assets to innovate but we also have some people who are not and unfortunately the people who seem less interested in supporting financial innovation are the ones that are in the room and those of us who are not in the room while this bill is being drafted are sort of left to see what it looks like after the less transparent discussions are taken care of.",
