  {
    "episode_id": 45973,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45973.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Well, I fear that their knowledge is very basic, that they're applying concepts to non-feot currency, that they're used to applying to fiat currency, and they don't understand adequately the difference between the two. And instead of letting Congress take a lighter footprint, with regard to the statutory and regulatory environment. I think they're taking a heavy footprint in the name of tax collections. They're saying digital assets are not reported. We're losing lots of tax revenue. This is bleeding tax revenue. We need to pay for this bill. And it's caused them to have a major influence in the drafting of this bill thus far. So we're trying very hard to work with people who are more friendly to innovation in this space to push back. This is for many people a very new, very new concept, very new space. When I walk around here with books on digital assets, cryptocurrency, my colleagues look at me like I'm different. And it's because they haven't taken the time to explore this. It's viewed in Washington as a very minor issue within the financial regulatory space. And furthermore, they don't see how fast it's happening. It is happening faster than I thought it could when I first got here in January. And so not only are we not up to speed with The status of things today, we're not up to speed on the status of things a year ago. Now, think about how far behind the eight ball that puts a lot of people in the financial innovation space. Some people within the regulatory space at the Commodities Futures Trading Commission at the FDIC, at the SEC. and some people at the Fed seem to be catching up, but we're peddling the bicycle really fast just to catch up. And then now we've got treasury, which I do not believe is anywhere near as thoroughly versed on these issues as some of the agencies I already mentioned. They're the ones who now are legislating, influencing legislation on this subject within the Briggs and Mortar infrastructure bill. So I'm really worried about the influencing, they're having over the process and I'm delighted that Finance Committee Chairman Wyden is weighing in on this. I hope he's very successful.",
