  {
    "episode_id": 45973,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45973.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Well, that's a great question. We're still trying to develop an understanding of what's happening on these issues ourselves. I have not personally reached out to her. I think that her remarks are somewhat alarming. So I hope to schedule a meeting with her about this, but I haven't done so yet. I'm also hopeful that her Democrat colleagues like Senator Wyden are reaching out to her to have a interrupt party discussion about these issues. But we're legislating, in my opinion, in this bill, on cryptocurrency before we're ready to. And I would rather see the committee process work here. On the banking committee, we seem to have developed a level of understanding about the importance of taking a light touch in terms of statutory and regulatory frameworks, at least for now, notwithstanding the Senator from Massachusetts. And we're working with the House on a bipartisan basis on these issues as well. And it's also true on the Commerce Committee. So when you have Senator Tumi from banking, you have Senator Wyden, the Chairman of the Finance Committee, And you have Marsha Blackburn on the Commerce Committee and others weighing in on this. It gives us an opportunity to cast a wide net in understanding where policy might get made on these subjects and to make sure that the people on the committees that are likely to have jurisdiction have a knowledge base that is shared among both parties. So that's the goal. We're not there yet. And that's why I hate to see The subject of digital asset and the taxation components of it get a little convoluted in this bricks and mortar infrastructure bill.",
