    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45973.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "life. And I feel like my own savings are being eroded when inflation goes up, when we debase the value of the US dollar. And so one of the reasons I became so interested in digital currency was because it's not tied to the US dollar. It's not tied to any fiat currency. And so to me, there's a comfort level in knowing there's some store of value out there that's not tied. to the U.S. dollar or another fiat currency because the Congress is spending too much money. It is fueling inflation. We're seeing it right now. We have seen since the turn of the century that at one point we were below 5 trillion in debt. And now we're bumping up against 30 trillion in debt. We're spending money at 100% of GDP. Our debt is 100% of GDP. My gosh, that is a tipping point for trouble. And we just seem to blow through these numbers as if they were insignificant. So I've been the first time I ran for Congress, which was in 2008. I did it because I was worried about our debt. And at that time, our debt was a little less than $10 trillion. And now we're just going to blow through $30 trillion like it didn't matter. So this has always been an issue that I've been concerned about. You know, I look at people who are working really hard every day, only to find out that their dollar is worth less than it was when they first earned it. And it's cruel. And it's really, it's most cruel to people who are working paycheck to paycheck. And so I just think it's a healthier America If people who are saving for their future and responsible with their money are rewarded for that, but the way that Congress is treating money, it is a disincentive to save money in U.S. dollars. And I just, I want to fight it. I'm going to keep fighting it. At the same time, I'm kind of relieved that there's this safety net out there, this safe harbor that is a non fiat currency.",
