  {
    "episode_id": 45973,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45973.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, certainly the most immediate concern that I have is inflation. We had terrible inflation when I was first out of college and got into debt to buy some agricultural land. And I personally guaranteed the loan. I also mortgaged the property. And in the early 1980s, we were paying 14% interest on agricultural land. And we barely were able to make the payments at that kind of an interest rate. And non-agloans were even higher than that. So when I was a young person in agriculture, there were people whose families were having to just turn the keys to the ranch right back to the bank and move it into town and do jobs that they didn't want to do, but they just needed the work. And so I've seen the effects of runaway inflation. and the high interest rates that tend to accompany them, because what, of course, the Fed would try to do when you've got an overheated economy and runaway inflation is try to slow the economy down, to cool it off, and it has a devastating effect. So whether you've got a home mortgage or a business mortgage, or in my case, a ranch mortgage, it just It's demoralizing. I don't know what else to say. It's truly demoralizing. And so since I've been through it myself, I have a real personal and ain't fear of inflation. So that's my most immediate concern. The long-term concern is that here's people putting money in a 401k or a keel plan and doing the responsible thing. And yet the money isn't going to be worth as much when they begin to retire and take it out. So they're always, always pursuing a goal that they can never meet because the value of the money they're saving is worth less and less over time. And I just, those are the two cruel realities of an irresponsible Congress.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
