    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 45973,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45973.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "Well, I'm very worried because I think that's what I'm seeing when I go home on the weekends. I have a friend who's a home builder, a home that costs him $305,000 all in to build and put on the market last year, just a year ago. Now costs him $180,000 to build and put on the market. Now think about if you're a young homeowner and The same house, the very same house, has gone up $75,000 in one year. Well, let's say you only make $75,000 in a year, which is more than the average salary in my home state. It begins to put it out of reach in a very short time. And one of the things that has kept these houses available to young home buyers is interest rates. But if interest rates have to go up, It's going to put those homes out of reach. So you'll have the combination of higher interest rates and higher home costs. And we'll have fewer in people. with fewer and fewer people who were able to afford them. The same is true with new cars, the same is true with used cars, the same is true with renting cars, the same is true with hotels, the same is true with vacations, airfare. With everything going up, including food, the disposable income just doesn't go as far as it has.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
