  {
    "episode_id": 45973,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45973.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "I would love it if he would come on your podcast and you would ask him those questions. You know, originally when he said he thought it inflation was transitory, I think he believed it. I think he's changing his tune on that. I think he is now more concerned that it's not transitory, that it is going to stick around for a while. There has been no indication that that is precipitating a change in policy at the Fed. But I think they're starting to realize it's happening. I think when you look at statistics, like he looks at every day, he's looking at data. He's looking at price indices. It's a little bit like if you go to the doctor and the doctor tells you what's wrong with you without asking you how you feel. He's just looking at your charts. He's paying no attention to you as a person. He's looking at your charts. I kind of think that's the way that Jay Powell is here. He's looking at the charts and he's not going to the grocery store. He's not going to the gas station. He's not trying to buy a home. And because of that, he is looking at statistics that are not reflective of boots on the ground experience out in our states. And that's why we kept trying to tell him, Jay, prices are higher now in April than they were in March. And then the next month, the same was true. And then in June, even higher than in May. and much higher. In fact, I think that inflation in June was the highest one-month inflation that we've had in like 30 years. I think he's starting to see it now, but those of us who are just living our lives and talking to people who are living our normal lives really are the ones who are feeling it directly and feeling it early.",
